    "lawCode": "Amd §1616, N-PC L",
    "lawSection": "Not-for-Profit Corporation Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7171\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the not-for-profit corporation law, in relation to\nassignment of rights by a land bank\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThis bill authorizes not-for-profit Land Banks to assign all rights to\nreal property purchased at public sale, due to foreclosure of said\nproperty as a result of unpaid property taxes, to the foreclosing\ngovernmental entity.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one amends the not-for-profit corporation law governing\ndelinquent property tax enforcement to authorize an applicable Land\nBank to assign all rights resulting from the Land Bank's successful\ntender of real property to the foreclosing governmental unit.\n\nSection two establishes the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nNew York State's Land Bank program was developed in 2011 in an effort\nto combat the problem of vacant and abandoned properties by\nestablishing authority to acquire real property that is tax\ndelinquent, tax foreclosed, vacant and/or abandoned so that such\nproperties are returned to a productive use.\n\nOf particular significance, cities all across the State of New York\nhave seen an uptick in tax-delinquent properties, with certain\nmunicipalities with larger housing stocks, being adversely impacted\nmore so than others. For example, the City of Buffalo has foreclosed\non thousands of tax-delinquent properties through its In Rem\nForeclosure process. Unfortunately, many of these properties are\ntransferred from one delinquent property owner to another,\nperpetuating a cycle that stymies productive reuse of the property and\nrevitalization of the surrounding neighborhood in which the property\nis located.\n\nIn order to improve the effectiveness of this worthwhile program, this\nlegislation would add another tool to a Land Bank's proverbial tool\nbox, in essence, returning the identified real property back over to\nthe foreclosing governmental entity, thereby providing a vehicle in\nwhich said property can be renovated or repurposed with the ultimate\ngoal of restoring to the tax rolls.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
